16-Sep-2026  0.92.1 Connects to more servers. A server set to Standard (legacy)
                    RDP Security only refuses the TLS offer (negotiation failure
                    code 2); instead of failing, the client now retries
                    automatically with Standard security and connects. A new
                    'Standard security only (no TLS)' option (the -S flag, and a
                    checkbox in the connection editor) lets you force that. The
                    RDP negotiation trace is now shown by the -v option, so
                    connection problems are easier to diagnose. The fallback
                    only happens when the server explicitly refuses TLS, never
                    on a failed TLS handshake.

16-Sep-2026  0.92.0 Added a connection manager. The iconbar Connections menu
                    now lists your saved connections, with 'New connection' at
                    the top; each can be connected to, edited or deleted, and
                    a delete asks first. A new editor sets the server, port,
                    user, password, domain, screen size, colours, display mode
                    and speed, so you no longer have to hand-write a connection
                    Obey file. Resolution and colours can be set to match the
                    RISC OS screen. Passwords are masked on screen, but are
                    saved as plain text in the connection file. Tab moves
                    between fields. Also fixes full-screen mode, which a saved
                    connection now honours, and the startup banner now credits
                    both authors and lists the -v trace option.

16-Sep-2026  0.91.1 Fixed connecting to Microsoft Windows servers over TLS.
                    Modern Windows enforces two RDP security requirements the
                    client was not meeting and dropped the connection during
                    setup. The decisive one is an anti-tamper check: the client
                    must confirm the security protocol the server chose, so a
                    'man in the middle' cannot downgrade it - the client now
                    does. It also advertises no RDP-layer encryption under TLS,
                    as required. Non-Windows TLS servers (xrdp, NuoRDS) were
                    never affected. TLS errors are now reported with the
                    underlying AcornSSL message. A protocol trace for diagnosing
                    connection problems can be turned on with the -v option or
                    by setting the RDPClient$Debug system variable; it is off by
                    default.

15-Sep-2026  0.91   Added RDP-over-TLS support using the RISC OS AcornSSL
                    module. When AcornSSL is present the client offers TLS in
                    the RDP negotiation and, if the server selects it, upgrades
                    the connection to a TLS session before login - so it can
                    now connect to servers that require TLS (modern Windows,
                    and TLS-configured xrdp / NuoRDS). A server that only does
                    standard RDP security still connects as before, and without
                    the AcornSSL module the client is unchanged. The Status
                    window shows "TLS" while a TLS session is active. This
                    is TLS encryption only, not TLS/NLA: Network Level
                    Authentication (CredSSP) is not supported. When the
                    server presents its certificate AcornSSL shows a
                    dialogue asking the user to accept or reject it, so
                    a self-signed certificate can be accepted
                    interactively. It also fixes a null-pointer crash in
                    the display poller that could abort the client when a
                    connection dropped or was re-established while the
                    socket poll loop was still running.

14-Sep-2026  0.90.3 Pasting text from a RISC OS application into the remote
                    session now works (the RISC OS -> server clipboard
                    direction). The client offers both CF_TEXT and
                    CF_UNICODETEXT and converts RISC OS text to UTF-16LE with a
                    single NUL terminator when the server asks for Unicode
                    (xrdp / Windows / NuoRDS); an earlier double terminator was
                    tolerated by xrdp but rejected by NuoRDS/macOS. Clipboard
                    data offered by an app in any filetype is now accepted,
                    requesting text on save, so word-processor sources such as
                    Writer+ are handled where they can supply plain text.
                    Completes the two-way clipboard begun in 0.90.2. The
                    -Otime speed optimisation (removed in 0.90.1) is
                    re-enabled, safe now that -za1 forces aligned code.

